# LC input streams and standing output questions · `spec:nrz:lc-streams`

**Principle (One indexed evaluation over finite and open axes)** · `prin:nrz:lc-stream-contract`

A stream specializes the two graphs joined by bindings in (`prin:nrz:lc-grid-contract`), (`prin:nrz:descriptor-binding-two-graphs`), (`spec:nrz:descriptor-heap-computation`). The common indexed domains, exact certificates, native input/output bindings, LC projections, decision evidence and graph-cost model are defined in (`spec:nrz:lc-grid-tiles`), especially (`def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-tile-certificate`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-output-binding`), (`def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`). This document adds the open axis, arrivals, standing questions and source refinement, and owns the host-memory loan and bounded-retirement mechanism for both streams and finite grids. Values vary in the computational graph; checked decision nodes remain the sole path from heap results to new semantic descriptor facts. These are target contracts and open capabilities, not claims that the present LC adapters implement streams.

## Open axis and arrivals · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-axis-contract`

**Definition (Open axis, epoch and clock relation)** · `def:nrz:lc-stream-axis`

A stream axis is an exact descriptor of an ordered, append-only index domain with no declared final extent. Its published prefix domains grow through inclusion edges; a prior prefix is immutable. Indexed input symbols, an epoch node and the declared clock relation connect each sample's reported time and physical time to the original LC relation. An index denotes order and sample association, not a float timestamp or an inferred physical count. The clock relation retains per-sample timestamp support and the one shared constant offset under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`). Appending adds region versions over new axis segments, with the bindings of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-array-binding`); it does not construct a new LC relation for each reading. A standing output may use this same axis without requiring a numerically valued input for every possible index.

**Definition (Segment and its published restriction)** · `def:nrz:lc-stream-segment`

A stream tile is a bounded segment subdomain node of the open axis, with checked inclusion and boundary-ownership edges under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-domain`). Its reserved extent is distinct from its published filled extent. Consecutive published segments have ordered, disjoint index domains; their cover edges describe the published prefix, including explicit gap or unavailable subdomains where applicable. Reserving future memory does not assert that samples exist there. Parameterized segment inclusion and certificate restrictions are prepared before filling; concrete segment-domain setup, if needed, is charged as setup under (`def:nrz:lc-stream-cost`). Physical support boxes can overlap without duplicating axis ownership. A finite grid tile uses the same restriction and binding mechanism, with a finite parent and the sub-box geometry of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-tile`).

**Rule (Ordering, gaps, late arrivals and duplicates)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-arrival-order`

The axis declaration chooses source order and its relation to acquisition order; physical timestamps can have overlapping uncertain supports without changing that declared order. A bounded reorder subdomain may precede publication when the source arrives out of order. Advancing past an absent source index requires an explicit gap subdomain and its missing-input meaning; there is no interpolation or invented observation. A declared completion frontier states which earlier indices will no longer receive ordinary first publications. A later arrival for such an index cannot splice or overwrite the published prefix: it is refused as late, or enters a separately admitted correction question with a reference to the earlier domain. A duplicate association refers to the same source-axis selection and is marked as a duplicate event, never another independent measurement or another counted answer. Conflicting bytes for that association require a correction or refusal. Reorder, gap, duplicate and late outcomes use predeclared domain/outcome families; their concrete domain preparation and retention costs remain visible. The policy choice is (`open:nrz:lc-stream-order-policy`).

**Rule (Time support and the clock-drift boundary)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-clock-boundary`

The epoch and clock relation remain attached to every segment, including windows and corrections. A constant offset is not a clock-rate parameter. Per-sample time intervals can enclose drift only over their declared observation window; they neither infer a drift law nor authorize extrapolation under (`claim:nrz:lc-closing-excludes-clock-drift`). An open index axis does not make a finite physical-time box, cycle cover or error goal valid forever. A new time range, changed epoch coordinates or new branch extent needs a checked domain restriction or admission with explicit original-epoch transport; it reuses unaffected relation, source, pi and root authority under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-shared-nodes`). If such evidence is unavailable, the relevant future segment is refused or remains explicitly undecided within an admitted domain. Rollover of storage or segment-local index representation does not reset physical time, uncertainty or the shared source.

## Preparation and host-filled tiles · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-preparation`

**Rule (Certify a declared support before readings arrive)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-support`

Before lending a tile, nrz admits a support-box node for the sensor's declared range, the complete per-sample deterministic uncertainty, the shared systematic-source domain and any time, positivity, cycle, branch and numerical-range restrictions. Their original equations and joint-domain edges follow (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`); statistical moments alone supply no box under (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-certified-sets`). The certificate and parameterized lowering of (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-tile-certificate`) quantify over every allowed reading in that support and the declared question schema. They are prepared once per admitted domain, independently of which readings arrive and how many compatible tiles use them. Existing rational-square-only covers do not certify general positive LC supports; the required root evidence and all projection directions remain (`req:nrz:lc-grid-irrational-cover`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`). A future tightening of the shared source is a separate refinement, not an assumption made during preparation.

**Rule (Native admission within a prepared domain)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-admission`

For each returned filled selection, native checks authenticate the loan, region and companion versions, source-family association, finite payloads and the complete input support including conversion error against the prepared box and its admitted numerical guards. This instantiates the already checked family binding under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-array-binding`); it adds no exact rational literal, scalar descriptor, CAS invocation or exact Work per in-support arrival. Passing containment authorizes that reading under the existing premises, not a new inferred relation fact. Changed meanings or sources cannot be authenticated by equal float bytes. Checking only a center is insufficient. A sample that would tighten a shared-source constraint may be evaluated under the old valid support; publishing that tighter semantic constraint requires the separate checked feedback of (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-refinement`).

**Rule (Out-of-support outcomes and new admission)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-out-of-support`

An input support outside the declared box produces an out-of-support event on its segment and has no answer certified by that box. Predeclared outcomes allow a typed refusal node with unavailable output, or a request to prepare a new admitted domain node and its certificate before evaluating the retained input. If the original physical question is admitted but a needed cover or cut cannot be certified within its allowance, an undecided stratum retains that new domain's open obligations under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`); it is not an answer borrowed from the failed old containment check. Physical malformedness and representation failure remain refusals. New admission reuses the original relation, source identities and every bracket, pi, cover or lowering proof whose premises still hold; widened support cannot inherit narrower authority without a proof. A numerical failure selects a prepared refusal path; any mathematical new-domain or impossibility assertion still crosses the checked decision boundary. Retrying or admitting a new box consumes a declared budget and may apply backpressure. Silent extrapolation and automatic widening of published premises are forbidden.

**Rule (Pending input versions and exclusive host loans)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-input-loan`

nrz allocates a tile's input and required companion regions, binds them to the indexed input symbols, prepares the support certificate and reserves the output bindings before any reading arrives. The computational graph contains a pending input-region version with a loan edge to the host borrower. Only that borrower can write the lent selections; nrz cannot read them, validate their payloads or run consuming kernel edges while the loan is outstanding. The host fills them from its sensor outside the engine. The loan carries the prepared capacity, source/axis associations and required companion coverage. In Rust it is an ordinary safe exclusive borrow or an owned, non-cloneable handle returned to nrz: safe initialization and the ownership transfer require neither unsafe code nor a copy of the filled numerical storage. This is the same mechanism for a host filling a finite grid tile. Loan cancellation makes its unpublished selections unavailable and releases the reservation only after exclusive ownership returns.

**Rule (Handback, validation and input publication)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-handback`

Handback closes the loan and gives nrz exclusive access to validate the filled selections under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-admission`). Successful handback publishes the complete input/companion version bundle over the admitted filled subdomain; only after that publication may kernel-application edges consume it in (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-heap-pass`). Returning ownership alone is not successful publication. Foreign, stale, multiply returned or incomplete bundles are refused before consumption. An out-of-support selection follows (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-out-of-support`); valid selections may proceed only through declared subdomain restrictions with explicit coverage of the unavailable remainder. Publication cannot hide a failed selection inside a supposedly complete segment. The fallible checks precede the atomic commit, with no host callback inside it under (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-atomic-publication`). Output publication is a subsequent complete binding bundle under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-publication`).

**Rule (Partial tiles publish only filled prefixes)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-partial-tile`

A host may return a tile before it is full, including to satisfy a latency bound. The filled prefix is a subdomain node with an inclusion edge to the reserved segment; its binding selects only initialized input and companion entries. The unfilled suffix has no published input version, is never read by containment checks or kernels, and is not automatically a gap or a lost sample. Capacity can be reused for a later segment after safe retirement, without treating the earlier reservation as evidence of arrival. An empty handback cancels or renews the reservation according to its declared policy and publishes no answers. Sparse arrivals use the explicit gap/reorder domains of (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-arrival-order`), rather than representing holes as filled numerical entries. Any new exact restriction setup is separately charged; a prefix selection covered by prepared family evidence does not rebuild the certificate.

**Rule (Several tiles in flight within one capacity)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-inflight`

Distinct pending and published region versions allow the host to fill one tile while nrz evaluates the preceding tile. Loan edges and kernel dependencies forbid either party from accessing the other's exclusively owned selections. The capacity bound includes every lent, queued, validating, computing, completed-but-unconsumed and refining tile, together with its companions and scratch; the bound is not merely the current kernel's tile size. A buffer is lent again only after all live consumers release the previous occupation, and a new generation cannot authenticate an old binding. Exhausted capacity stops new loans or uses a declared dropped-input policy under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-backpressure`). Overlapping fill and evaluation reduces pipeline idle time without changing the certificate, publication order or per-reading latency definition.

**Rule (Tile size trades fill latency against setup cost)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-tile-size`

For a filled tile of b readings and exact setup cost W_setup attributable to that tile, amortized exact setup Work per reading is `W_setup/b`; after preparation, the heap pass still has zero exact Work per admitted reading. A reused certificate contributes its cost once across the readings it covers, not once per tile; restriction and refinement costs are reported separately. A small tile normally returns sooner after its first reading. A large tile can amortize setup and scheduling over more readings, but takes longer for the sensor to fill. With no queue, first-reading latency is fill time plus validated pass/publication time; at constant rate r the ideal fill time from the first to the last of b readings is `(b-1)/r`. A partial return uses its actual filled population in the denominator and its actual fill time. Capacity, certificate validity and error growth constrain b under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-size`). Host-filled grids share this mechanism; their fill time is the host's preparation time rather than an assumed sensor cadence.

## Standing answers and decisions · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-answers`

**Definition (Standing output symbol)** · `def:nrz:lc-stream-standing-question`

A standing question is an output symbol indexed over the open axis and attached to the original LC relation, supplied/query roles and explicit question domain. Lowering binds it to a stream of result-region versions restricted to segment subdomains, with deterministic-bound and numerical-error companion symbols and any required branch/sample axes under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-output-binding`). The parameterized binding family exists before arrival; each segment's concrete result and companion reservations exist before its kernel pass. Asking for phase at each new reading uses (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-forward-series`); asking whether that phase is still below a threshold additionally declares the predicate, boundary ownership and quantifier of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`). A per-reading question, a question over a closed window and an aggregate over the growing prefix are distinct domain edges. The latter two cannot acquire their meanings merely from the most recent answer.

**Rule (Complete, atomic and ordered segment publication)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-publication`

A published answer segment makes every requested output selection and its required companions available together, or identifies that selection's explicit undecided, domain or refusal outcome. Checked disjoint cover edges account for its whole published index domain, including declared gaps; completeness means no unreported hole, not successful numerical answers for missing inputs. Atomic binding publication follows (`rule:nrz:descriptor-binding-atomic-publication`), and selected decision evidence follows (`rule:nrz:decision-selected-publication`). Consumers resolve answers by output symbol, segment-domain selection and published version, never by buffer address. Initial segments are exposed in axis order; a bounded holdback frontier prevents a faster later tile from implying completion of an earlier one. Refinements and invalidations name their earlier version and have dependency order after it, even when their target is an older segment. A consumer can distinguish an initial answer, a refinement, an invalidation and a gap by those graph edges. Failure publishes no incomplete answer bundle. An unfinished open axis has no whole-stream completion claim; close requires a terminal-domain edge and complete accounting through that extent.

**Rule (Decision strata with open extent)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-strata`

A decision node over the open axis partitions each published domain into strata using (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-cell-decisions`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-region-decisions`), (`spec:nrz:lattice-decisions`). An open stratum may continue across newly published segments while its predicate, source dependencies and checked evidence remain compatible. Each extension is an immutable larger-domain version with inclusion, coverage and evidence edges; no prior stratum is silently enlarged and no old proof authenticates unseen inputs. An open extent is distinct from an undecided truth status: all currently covered indices can have certified membership while future membership remains unknown. A changed branch, declared window end or closed stream can seal an extent; resolving its truth obligations settles its verdict. An undecided stratum retains its last valid enclosure, open obligations and typed stop reason under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-stop`); a pending-future domain edge additionally distinguishes missing observations from exhausted proof work. More data offers a later refinement opportunity, not an unlimited retry allowance or a promise of eventual decision. Continuations depend on checked selection in a strictly later stratum under (`rule:nrz:decision-stratification`).

**Rule (Finality names both domain and assumptions)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-finality`

A decision can be final for a closed segment and immutable assumption snapshot once its obligations settle; that says nothing about every future reading or a changed source model. For a growing prefix, an all-readings predicate such as phase below a threshold has only a provisional yes from observed coverage; a checked counterexample can settle no for every extension that retains it. An existential predicate can settle yes from a retained witness, while observed exclusion alone cannot settle no for an open future. The opposite final conclusions require a closed domain or a checked invariant over every future input allowed by the admitted support and clock model. Physical uncertainty introduces a separate quantifier under (`def:nrz:lc-grid-predicate`): a witness used for an existential assertion or a counterexample may disappear when the joint feasible set tightens. Finality across such refinements therefore requires a persistence proof, not merely an append-only axis. Universal coverage and complete exclusion transport through checked inclusion when their nonemptiness/domain premises still hold; empty refined physical sets have explicit impossibility outcomes. Moving-window decisions are final only for each closed window version. These scopes prevent an early provisional answer from selecting an irreversible future continuation without its required proof.

## Shared sources and refinement · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-sources`

**Definition (One systematic source over the stream)** · `def:nrz:lc-stream-shared-source`

A shared systematic error is one source node for the whole stream, with immutable versions of its support and joint constraints. Per-sample uncertainty remains on the indexed input family; segments do not create independent copies of the shared source. Conditional fibres, clock/phase offsets and calibration gains retain the original equations and source-map edges under (`def:nrz:descriptor-binding-source-symbols`), (`rule:nrz:lc-relation-two-layer-measurements`). New observations may constrain this same source. Their arrival alone does not replace its declared support, and marginal bounds cannot prove joint feasibility. A physical recalibration or changed error law requires an explicitly related new assumption domain, not an unlabelled mutation of the old source.

**Rule (Monotone information is published by refinement edges)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-refinement`

A source refinement admits a new joint feasible domain with checked inclusion in the old domain. If proposed from numerical observations, decision nodes validate the corresponding evidence before a later descriptor snapshot is lowered. For the same output selection, publish a new answer version with refinement edges to the old version and the inclusion proof, satisfying (`req:nrz:lc-relation-refinement-monotonicity`): the new covering set is contained in the old, branch selections map to old covered branches, and proved information is retained. Intersection with prior coverage may be needed; it must retain the same-target and joint-domain evidence. A nonempty refinement cannot move a certified horizon earlier; emptiness has no physical horizon. Exhausted refinement leaves earlier coverage valid and an explicit unresolved obligation. The old publication is immutable and remains a valid, possibly wider historical enclosure. Refinement work is explicit exact work at the refinement boundary, not hidden inside the zero-exact-work arrival path. A tighter source version may refine earlier answers and future ones; recomputation follows only affected dependency edges and its scheduling is bounded.

**Rule (Refinement, stale attachments and changed assumptions)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-invalidation`

Appending disjoint inputs leaves independent historical answers untouched. Compatible narrowing preserves their old coverage as information under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-refinement`), but live bindings or decision attachments tied to a replaced source version must still be revalidated under (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-lifetime`); information preservation is not live-version authentication. New refined versions cite the prior answer and inclusion evidence. Replacing a reading without an inclusion proof, widening support, changing the epoch/map/source law, or losing required version authenticity invalidates the transitive live dependency closure under (`rule:nrz:decision-invalidation`); a new question/version cites an invalidation or supersession edge, not a monotone-refinement edge. Unaffected disjoint selections survive only with proven unchanged dependencies; otherwise conservative whole-family invalidation applies. Consumers see whether old coverage remains valid under a refinement or is unavailable for the current assumptions after invalidation, and can still resolve its historical theorem under (`rule:nrz:decision-attachment-lifetime`). No event overwrites published values or turns an old theorem into evidence for a new snapshot.

## Bounded residence and retained evidence · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-resources`

**Rule (Declared bounds on both live graphs)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-bounded-resources`

The execution policy bounds live heap bytes, resident descriptor nodes and bytes, referenced proof closures, pending loans, in-flight segments, reorder/holdback domains, source-refinement frontier and attempts independently of stream duration. It also bounds index representation, proof bit size, output-family extent and scratch. New domain nodes cannot bypass those ceilings by retaining earlier closures through hidden references. Compatible segments share original relation, source and certificate authority; sealed segments and obsolete source snapshots retire under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-retirement`). There is no descriptor or CAS construction for each in-support arrival after preparation. New exact growth is charged and capped per admitted domain/restriction or refinement under (`def:nrz:lc-stream-cost`). Successive exceptional domains can produce unbounded cumulative construction over an unbounded run, so cumulative counts and exported storage are reported separately from bounded resident size. Indefinitely retaining arbitrary distinct historical answers and evidence requires growing external storage; this contract does not claim bounded total historical information. A finite export allowance that fills forces backpressure or an explicit refusal before required evidence becomes unresolvable. The finite-grid application is (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-streaming`).

**Rule (Retirement preserves resolvable evidence paths)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-retirement`

Before retiring a segment's numerical regions and descriptor closure, retain or export the immutable path from its output symbols and published versions through segment domains, original inputs/assumptions, source versions, enclosure/cut evidence and certificate authority. Exported graph references must resolve to the actual scoped evidence; an opaque digest with unavailable premises does not suffice. Numerical storage may retire without retaining every live region version, and historical proof resolution never authenticates a reused buffer. The consumer's retention contract states whether the old numerical enclosure or only its evidence remains retrievable. Source refinements of retired outputs require bounded restoration of the relevant retained inputs/evidence, or a declared refinement window; missing input history makes recomputation unavailable without invalidating an otherwise valid old enclosure. Retained aggregate constraints need a proved sufficient summary or resolvable original dependencies, not silent forgetting. Each restoration is capacity-checked. Bounded segment-local index representations may roll over through prepared axis/epoch inclusion relations, with the longer history externally resolvable; exact transport and growing endpoint bit size remain charged and can refuse at their limits.

**Definition (Window as a subdomain, not a new source)** · `def:nrz:lc-stream-window`

A window is a declared subdomain node of the open axis, selected by an index interval or an admitted clock-domain predicate, with inclusion and boundary-ownership edges. Moving the window creates a new domain version and expires only the dependencies whose scope permits it. A window does not reset the systematic source or erase constraints from a whole-history inference. Dropping old constraints changes that inference question unless a checked sufficient-summary edge preserves it; any resulting widening is not monotone refinement. An event-time window with uncertain timestamps retains its boundary uncertainty and late-arrival policy. Closing a window requires its completion frontier and gap accounting before a final aggregate is available. The retention/refinement scope and the mathematical inference scope are separate declared domains.

**Rule (Backpressure and dropped input are visible graph outcomes)** · `rule:nrz:lc-stream-backpressure`

If no prepared capacity is available, nrz withholds a new loan and attaches a pending/backpressure outcome to the affected acquisition or requested domain; it does not allocate beyond the bound. A consumer holding output versions can therefore stall future acquisition. If the host or source cannot wait, the declared loss policy attaches a dropped-input gap subdomain with source/clock association and reason, and publishes its missing-output outcome in order. Unknown loss extent is explicitly an unresolved domain, not a fabricated exact gap count. No dropped reading becomes an independent zero or an observed value. Aggregate decisions over required but missing inputs remain undecided unless their evidence already covers all admissible values on that domain. Admission/refinement/export queues have the same bounded policy. Closing the stream resolves every pending loan and domain obligation to a published outcome or explicit stop; it cannot discard an outstanding borrower or an unfinished answer silently.

## Cost and acceptance evidence · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-acceptance`

**Definition (Exact growth and native work on a finite prefix)** · `def:nrz:lc-stream-cost`

Use the common accounting of (`def:nrz:lc-grid-cost`) on an observed prefix. Attribute exact nodes, evidence edges, descriptor constructions, CAS calls and Work to admitted support-domain preparation, segment/domain restriction setup and each refinement or failed attempt. Distinguish new authority from referenced authority and charge validation/reuse work even when it adds no node. Preparing a fresh time range, output branch extent, gap/correction domain or source refinement is not free arrival work. After preparation, each in-support arrival has zero exact Work, exact node/edge additions, descriptor constructions and CAS calls; containment, loan/version validation and indexed arithmetic are native work. For fixed question, output arity and bounded branch/sample extent, total heap work is linear in the number of evaluated readings, with cost for refused/dropped/duplicate handling and refinements reported separately. The computational graph grows by native region-version and kernel-application instances of the retained schedule, not a cloned exact question per reading. Exact setup divided by filled population follows (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-tile-size`); cumulative construction, peak resident graphs and external history are distinct quantities. No uniform constant-time guarantee covers an ever-growing cycle domain or exact all-history inference without its own bounded summary proof.

**Definition (Arrival-to-answer latency is its own measurement)** · `def:nrz:lc-stream-latency`

For each reading, latency runs from host-observed sensor arrival to visibility of its complete answer or explicit outcome. With no waiting queue it is the remaining tile fill time plus the pass time, where pass time includes handback validation, heap evaluation and atomic publication. General latency additionally includes bounded reorder/queue/holdback delay, any requested admission/refinement wait and consumer delivery delay; report those components separately. From the tile's first reading the fill component is its whole fill time, while its last reading has almost none. A monotonic host timing source measures these intervals independently of the uncertain physical clock relation; subtracting uncertain reported sensor timestamps does not establish latency. Throughput counts fully published readings per elapsed time and is not a substitute for latency. Report both time to the first valid answer and time to any requested refined or final decision, including unresolved waits and stopped outcomes.

**Criterion (Structural stream acceptance precedes speed targets)** · `crit:nrz:lc-stream-structural-acceptance`

Pass requires one prepared certificate reused across arbitrarily extended in-support controls, zero exact arrival-path counters under (`def:nrz:lc-stream-cost`), and duration-independent resident heap and descriptor bounds at fixed capacity. It also requires exclusive loans with no engine access while lent, no reads of unfilled suffixes, complete atomic companion publication, ordered once-only initial answers, explicit gaps/duplicates/out-of-support outcomes, and resolvable evidence after retirement. Shared-source mutations must reach exactly the proved dependency closure, monotone refinements must preserve information, and final decisions must meet (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-decision-finality`). A per-reading exact graph disguised as singleton segments fails. Optional exact domain/refinement operations remain charged and bounded; exporting an unbounded resident closure does not erase its measured storage cost. Semantic oracles, adversarial versions and boundary cases must pass alongside these counters. Latency and throughput are reported beside these pass/fail results; no fast result compensates for structural failure. These criteria describe future acceptance, not measurements achieved by this concept.

**Requirement (Synthetic sensor workload and independent controls)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-benchmark`

Extend the release `f64`, single-core conditions and repeated-timing protocol of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-benchmark`) to synthetic sensor prefixes at its populations, keeping event generation reproducible and distinct from measured engine work. Identify actual hardware, toolchain, arithmetic revisions, source cadence and burst pattern, tile capacities, queue limits and all admission/refinement/publication policies. Include a long uniform-support stream, generic positive non-square LC inputs, boundary/equality cases, out-of-support readings, a checked tightening of one shared systematic source that affects earlier outputs, declared gaps, repeated duplicates, late/conflicting arrivals, partial/empty handbacks, several concurrent loans and a stalled consumer. Include unchanged-center companion changes, source/map mutations, representation rollover and exhausted admission/refinement/export allowances. Exercise phase-per-reading, a standing threshold decision, closed-window and growing-prefix aggregates; retain the LC question-family obligations of (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`). Independent original-relation oracles check both uncertainty layers and original branch identities. Include a host-filled finite-grid control through the identical loan mechanism, and both supported numerical formats for correctness. No new measured numbers are asserted; the existing baseline and distinct whole-cell/cover-only Work slices remain (`obs:nrz:lc-grid-forward-cost`).

**Requirement (Graph counts, coverage and scaling evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-counters`

Apply (`req:nrz:lc-grid-fit`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-counters`) separately to preparation and already-prepared native passes, fitting engine work time `t(N)=k+c*N^p` with uncertainty intervals and raw observations while source pacing is measured separately. Show exact growth per admitted domain, restriction and refinement, including failed attempts, and prove the zero arrival-path counters on stable-support controls. Report prefix length, filled segment population, reused certificates, native region versions/kernel edges and peak heap, resident descriptor and retained/exported evidence sizes. Fixed-capacity duration sweeps must include pending loans, old shared-source closures, historical proof references and consumer holdback, so an unbounded reference chain cannot masquerade as bounded storage. Checked disjoint domain covers account for completed, undecided, refused and gap subdomains; duplicate transport events refer back to their original selections and add no second answer. Failed and unresolved offers remain in elapsed-time denominators, with offered, admitted and published populations distinguished by their graph domains. Attribute decision growth to the unresolved boundary/stratum evidence under (`req:nrz:lc-grid-boundary-measurement`), not an exact decision object for every settled interior reading.

**Requirement (Latency distributions and throughput beside structure)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-latency-evidence`

At each reported tile size and source cadence, measure the components of (`def:nrz:lc-stream-latency`), sustained published throughput, partial-fill frequency, queue occupancy, backpressure duration and dropped-input extent. Publish raw independent runs, confidence level, interval method and latency distribution including tail quantiles and maximum observed delay; unfinished outcomes are reported as censored waits or stops, not deleted observations. Vary burstiness and consumer speed separately from computational load. Compare first-valid and refined/final-answer latency, direct setup time and amortized setup Work, steady heap time, peak memory and retained graph growth. Fit linear full-output cost only at fixed numerical/output complexity with informative intervals under (`crit:nrz:lc-grid-structural-acceptance`). Use the comparable uncertified baseline and non-blocking ratio policy of (`crit:nrz:lc-grid-ratio-target`); latency objectives remain explicit owner choices under (`open:nrz:lc-stream-latency-policy`).

## Open capabilities and ownership · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-open-capabilities`

**Requirement (Open-axis admission and domain evidence)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-axis-admission`

Open capability, owned by bindings for physical correspondence and CAS for exact authority: specialize (`req:nrz:lc-grid-tile-authority`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-indexed-binding`) to the axis, segment and clock domains of (`def:nrz:lc-stream-axis`), (`def:nrz:lc-stream-segment`). Prepare parameterized restrictions and support certificates before arrival; retain original source/epoch identities across compatible domains and charge new admission when premises change. Predeclare gap, duplicate, correction and out-of-support outcomes with honest coverage. Support finite-time horizons and representation rollover without inventing a drift law or an infinite-validity certificate. The native guard path and the exact admission path must remain separately auditable.

**Requirement (Safe loan and handback lifecycle)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-host-loans`

Open capability, owned by matrix for region ownership and bindings for indexed correspondence: realize (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-input-loan`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-handback`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-partial-tile`) through safe borrows or owned handles with no required numerical copy. Enforce the pending-version loan edge, exclusive borrower writes, unreadability while lent, prefix-only validation, foreign/stale handback refusal and complete companion publication. Support concurrent independent loans under one declared capacity and reuse the same mechanism for finite grids. Safe ownership is necessary but does not by itself prove sensor-source association, physical containment or certificate validity.

**Requirement (Standing output and scoped decision publication)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-standing-bindings`

Open capability, owned by bindings and the decision/plan owner with matrix publication: apply (`req:nrz:lc-grid-family-kernels`), (`req:nrz:lc-grid-region-evidence`) to the standing symbols and ordered result versions of (`def:nrz:lc-stream-standing-question`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-publication`). Declare complete companion/branch bindings before execution, extend open strata only with new coverage evidence, and expose undecided/final status with its exact domain and assumption scope. Whole-future conclusions require invariant evidence; ordinary prefix answers cannot supply it. Lower every selected continuation strictly after the decision that authorizes it.

**Requirement (Shared-source refinement and historical consumers)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-source-refinement`

Open capability, owned by CAS and bindings for inclusion and dependency evidence and the plan owner for bounded scheduling: retain the single source of (`def:nrz:lc-stream-shared-source`), publish the monotone refinements of (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-refinement`) and distinguish them from invalidations under (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-invalidation`). Support affected earlier answers through an explicit retention/refinement scope, with capacity-checked restoration or a declared unavailable-refinement outcome. Prove any all-history sufficient summary before retiring its original live dependencies. Equal centers, narrower marginal boxes or a newer calibration name cannot replace joint inclusion evidence or live attachment validation.

**Requirement (Bounded execution and resolvable retirement for both axes)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-bounded-execution`

Open capability, owned by matrix and the plan/batch owner with bindings for evidence resolution: enforce (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-bounded-resources`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-retirement`), (`rule:nrz:lc-stream-backpressure`) across both finite grids and open streams. Bound every live owner and pending frontier, share compatible authority, retire or export sealed closures without keeping hidden live chains, and preserve resolvable symbol/version/evidence paths. Declare external storage, index/proof-size and consumer-retention allowances separately; stop or backpressure before exhausting them. Window domains obey (`def:nrz:lc-stream-window`). This is the shared lifetime capability cited by (`req:nrz:lc-grid-bounded-execution`), not a second engine or an implementation-specific queue API.

**Requirement (Acceptance evidence and implementation boundary)** · `req:nrz:lc-stream-acceptance-evidence`

Open capability, owned by measurement and the participating engine owners: supply the synthetic source, independent semantic expectations, graph counters, lifetime/mutation controls and raw timing evidence of (`req:nrz:lc-stream-benchmark`), (`req:nrz:lc-stream-counters`), (`req:nrz:lc-stream-latency-evidence`). Report (`crit:nrz:lc-stream-structural-acceptance`) before performance targets and identify any unimplemented query or lifecycle obligation. Existing LC covers, scalar laws and native versioning are the ingredients described in (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-existing-covers`), (`rule:nrz:lc-grid-existing-execution`); they do not establish open-axis loans, standing publication or bounded descriptor retirement. This concept adds no code, tests, limits, defaults, dependency changes or literal Work pins.

## Choices requiring the owner · `sec:nrz:lc-stream-owner-choices`

**Open Question (Ordering, lateness and corrections)** · `open:nrz:lc-stream-order-policy`

Options are strict source-order acceptance with explicit gaps, bounded reordering before a declared completion frontier, or separate correction questions for late data. Recommended position: source order with an optional bounded reorder horizon, explicit gap outcomes and corrections admitted as separate versions after closure. Duplicate associations never add independent evidence. The owner chooses the horizon, source-order interface and correction delivery policy; none may rewrite a published prefix or conceal missing observations.

**Open Question (Latency objective and tile sizing)** · `open:nrz:lc-stream-latency-policy`

Options are fixed tile capacity, a fill deadline, or adaptive size under explicit memory and latency caps. Recommended position: a capped adaptive policy with a deterministic fixed-size override and partial-prefix handback at a caller-visible deadline. This complements (`open:nrz:lc-grid-size-policy`) while exposing actual fill/pass/queue delay and setup amortization. The owner supplies the objective, tail quantile and capacities after measurement; no numerical default or deadline guarantee is established here. Out-of-support admission and optional refinement have separately visible latency budgets.

**Open Question (Out-of-support admission policy)** · `open:nrz:lc-stream-support-policy`

Options are immediate refusal, a bounded queue for new-domain admission, or caller-prepared alternative domains. Recommended position: predeclared alternatives where available, then explicit refusal unless the caller opts into a finite new-admission budget. The original relation and valid authority remain reusable, while failed containment never authorizes extrapolation. The owner chooses exposed admission/retry policy and availability behavior; undecided is reserved for an admitted mathematical question with open evidence, not an excuse to run an unsupported box.

**Open Question (Refinement scope and finality interface)** · `open:nrz:lc-stream-refinement-policy`

Options are refining only resident outputs, a declared retrospective window, or externally retained whole-history refinement with bounded restoration. Recommended position: an explicit retrospective window and optional external history, always publishing versioned refinements and invalidations with domain/source dependencies. Expose first-valid and final-for-snapshot answers distinctly, with whole-future finality only when proved. The owner chooses the retention scope, restoration budget and subscription delivery interface; the monotonicity and live-authentication requirements remain mandatory. Finite proof/refinement policies follow (`open:nrz:lc-grid-refinement-policy`).

**Open Question (Backpressure, loss and evidence retention)** · `open:nrz:lc-stream-retention-policy`

Options are lossless backpressure, explicit loss at the host boundary, or both as caller-selected policies, with internal bounded retention or a resolvable external evidence graph. Recommended position: lossless backpressure when the source permits it and explicitly opted-in loss otherwise, plus bounded resident evidence and externally resolvable history under a declared quota. The owner chooses capacities, consumer-release obligations and evidence retention guarantees. Exhausted history storage cannot silently erase required evidence, and loss cannot count as a completed observation.

**Open Question (Measurement protocol and staged delivery)** · `open:nrz:lc-stream-evidence-policy`

Options are delivering the full standing-query/refinement model together or independently reviewed capability increments. Recommended position: use (`open:nrz:lc-grid-evidence-policy`) for the common reference environment, repeated-run uncertainty and correctness formats, and add declared sensor cadence, burstiness and consumer-rate controls. Review host loans and stable-support publication before retrospective refinement, while leaving full acceptance open until every required structural criterion has evidence. Choose latency/throughput reporting and statistical protocol before measurements; performance targets remain non-blocking beside the structural pass/fail results.
